Original commit body from c0385f2e733860532cef9942dc3ea39f91ee01c9:

WI-dojud. check_rust_analyzer_disclosure picked its message from `available`
(is the binary on PATH) alone, after a single guard on "does this repo contain
Rust". It never consulted the opt-in gate, so every Rust repo with
rust-analyzer installed got the "installed but NOT enabled" arm -- including
runs where --backend rust-analyzer was active and the SCIP path demonstrably
ran. Positive control from the same invocation that printed it: the emitted
survey carried 2 edges of type `references` with origin ["scip"], which only
the SCIP path produces; the tree-sitter run of the same crate has zero.

Repro closed behaviourally on the probe crate:
  before  enabled run: 1x "NOT enabled"    default run: 1x "NOT enabled"
  after   enabled run: 0x, says ACTIVE     default run: 1x "NOT enabled"

THE WARNING IS KEPT, deliberately, and this is the whole reason the fix is a
new argument rather than a suppression. Its payload is a security disclosure:
`rust-analyzer scip <workspace>` executes that workspace's build.rs and
expands its proc macros AS THE INVOKING USER, and the function's own docstring
records that an attempt to sandbox it was tried, measured, and reverted. A
nudge that fires when it is already moot teaches people to skim past the one
sentence that has to land. So the caveat now appears in every arm that speaks
-- and in the enabled arm it is stated in the PRESENT tense, because there it
describes what is happening rather than what would happen if you opted in.

`enabled` is resolved at the call site from HYPERGUMBO_RUST_ANALYZER, mirroring
how `available` is resolved there, which keeps the check itself pure and
testable in all four states. That env var is the single signal for both opt-in
routes because cli.py normalises --backend rust-analyzer into it during argv
pre-parse.

One duplication, disclosed: the authority for the truthy value set is
gate._TRUTHY_VALUES, which lives in the OPTIONAL rust-analyzer package, and
core must decide what to print whether or not that package is installed. Core
therefore carries a copy -- and because a copy can drift, a test pins the two
together so adding a value on one side fails here rather than silently in a
user's terminal.

Also in this commit, spec §Taint-flow-analysis: the two attribute-primitive
gaps it calls "pending" are closed. WI-vipur (scoped-path traversal mode) and
WI-gotuv (C identifier-reference matching) both landed; verified end-to-end
rather than from tracker status -- std::env::consts::OS classifies env_read,
bare stdin/stdout classify ipc_recv/logging. The adjacent claim that "the
rust-analyzer optional backend is unaffected by the tree-sitter gap and
continues to resolve Rust semantics fully" is replaced: attribute-reference
coverage is the TREE-SITTER analyzer's, the SCIP backend emits no
module_attr_ref edges at all, and the two coexist rather than substitute
(ADR-0012; WI-gojum), so backend choice does not affect this coverage in
either direction. The clause was scope creep past its own commit message --
which claimed only that the gap was a tree-sitter grammar artifact -- and was
written three months before the resolve-map fix that stopped finalize from
silently discarding the entire SCIP call graph.

